phi-adapter-onkyo (0.0~git202609080955.3d3452f-1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * No Qt. The loop, the timers and the strings come from phi-runtime through
    the SDK's loop execution backend; JSON is nlohmann, as in phi-core.
  * The eISCP frame scanner could stand still on a frame declaring zero sizes,
    taking the instance thread with it until the host gave up and leaked the
    instance. It always moves forward now, and an impossible length ends the
    scan rather than being stepped over.
  * The poll preemption the README described could never fire: the callbacks
    that would have interrupted a poll were posted to the thread the poll was
    blocking. Nothing blocks the loop any more.
  * An input code is two hex digits. Anything else used to go out as
    SLI<whatever> and come back a success nothing had confirmed.
  * A superseded volume write is answered as superseded, not as a success for
    a command that was never sent; the active-code list keeps its order; a
    host that is not yet an address is tried instead of dropped; a port stored
    as text is read as a port.
  * The 1750-line sidecar is twelve units, and the frame scanner, the queue
    and the reporting rules have tests that do not need a receiver.

 -- Marius Schumacher <marius.schumacher@phisys.com>  Tue, 08 Sep 2026 09:55:01 +0200

